## Chapter 1.7: Disappearance

The garbled text caught Luka off guard. In all his earlier visions, everything had seemed clear. Then again, they hadn’t been perfectly sharp. There had always been blurry patches he’d dismissed as mental haze—or as his brain adding a few effects to make the visions look cool and mysterious.

But this was different. The whole message had been unreadable. The only explanation he could come up with was that the future wasn’t fixed yet. But why would that make the words blur together?

“So… how was it? Did you see anything?” Nadya’s voice pulled him back.

Luka let the vision fade. “Unfortunately, it was all garbled. I couldn’t make out a thing.” He shook his head, trying to clear the last traces of it.

“Maybe this paper’s no good.” Nadya started rummaging through her bag. “I can try something else.”

Luka turned the problem over in his mind. Maybe he’d caught a moment before the case was solved. But that didn’t explain the overlapping text. Unless that was exactly what he was seeing: several possible futures at once.

The idea clicked into place. It was only a theory, but it was the best explanation he had.

“How about this?” Nadya held out a scrap of ordinary paper.

“I don’t think switching paper will help.” Luka nudged it back toward her. “I think I might know why it happened.”

Nadya and Lin turned to him.

Luka paused, running through the idea once more. “We thought my vision showed one straight, _linear_ future. But what if the future branches into several possibilities?”

He tapped the edge of the table. “My vision uses the past and present as a starting point, then looks ahead. This case is still unfolding. There could be several likely outcomes, and if they overlap in the vision, the text gets scrambled.”

Lin leaned in, following his explanation. “So, if the future’s more settled, the vision might be clearer?”

“That’s what I think. If Nadya writes our receipt number, for example, there’s less uncertainty than if she promises to write a case summary. The different possibilities might converge—or at least diverge less.” Luka glanced between them. “That’s the theory, anyway.”

“It makes sense,” Nadya said. She pulled the e-paper and stylus back toward her. “But let’s test it. I’ll write our receipt number after we pay.”

Luka focused on the paper. The vision came over him, and the handwriting blurred at the edges—but the overlapping lines were close enough that he could read the number. There were two more lines beneath it.

“Okay. The number’s 211804140266.” Luka looked up from the vision, a grin spreading across his face. “And you will write, ‘Hello, Luka from the past.’”

Lin laughed. Nadya tilted her head. “Really?”

“Hey, can I write something?” Lin asked.

“Sure.” Nadya passed her the stylus.

Lin quickly wrote a few Chinese characters in the corner. As she finished, Luka leaned over and whispered in Chinese, “And, Sis, you will write, ‘If you see this, send me Nadya’s contact later.’”

Lin’s face reddened. She clicked her tongue and gave him a playful punch on the arm. Nadya glanced between them, curious but oblivious to Lin’s crush.

“What did you write?” Nadya asked.

“Same thing as you, just in Chinese,” Lin said, then leaned over for a sip of her drink.

Nadya accepted that without question. “Maybe I should frame this at home. Then your vision can get a good look at it.”

Lin nearly spat out her drink. She couldn’t exactly ask Nadya not to frame her secret message without giving the game away.

After wiping her mouth with a napkin, Lin steered them back to the problem. “So much for cheating our way to the answer. What do we do now?”

Nadya set her drink down. “We visit Anatoley. I can look up his address.”

“Can you do that off-duty?” Luka asked. “Could it get you in trouble?”

“A couple of basic queries won’t set off any alarms. I can’t access his full biography without asking an archivist at the station, though.”

Nadya tapped through the public records on her pocket terminal. The listing gave them the basics: name, gender, age, occupation, phone number, email, and address. She paused at the map. His house was on the south side of town, while the scrapyard was to the north. Why had he ended up there so late at night?

Even though they already knew Anatoley was dead, a thin lead was still a lead. They decided to check his house.

After they paid, Nadya checked the café receipt. The number matched what Luka had seen, giving her a little more faith in his visions. She wrote the greeting he’d seen on the e-paper, rolled it up, and tucked it into her bag.

Fifteen minutes later, they got off the bus in the southern district. It was quieter here, with little traffic and few people about. Anatoley’s house was a block from the stop.

Snow covered most of the yard and walkway; it had barely been cleared. Anatoley didn’t seem to be the tidy type. They slowed as they reached the gate, careful not to step into the yard. Lin pointed toward the front door.

“There—footprints.”

A single pair of footprints led away from the door, then disappeared among the overlapping tracks on the sidewalk.

Lin raised her camera and took a photo, framing the trail before their own tracks could muddle it.

Only then did they cross the yard. Nadya, the only one of them with a Militsiya ID, rang the bell and waited. When no one answered, she tried the handle. The door was locked.

“No one’s home,” Nadya said quietly. She let out a sigh. “Rest in peace, Comrade Ruzhin.”

“At least we know he left the house,” Lin said, nodding toward the tracks.

Luka studied the trail. “I wonder if he went straight to the scrapyard.”

“You three looking for Anatoley?” a voice called from next door.

An old man stood behind his fence, one hand resting on the gate. He didn’t seem hostile, just curious.

Luka started to answer, but Lin beat him to it. “Hi. I’m Lin Chen, with the _Heilongjiang People’s Daily_.” She showed the man her press card. “My crew and I were hoping to interview Comrade Ruzhin, but he isn’t answering. Do you know where he might be?”

You had to hand it to Lin: years of reporting had made her quick on her feet.

“Ah, I see. I was just heading out for groceries when I saw you.” The neighbor laughed. “I didn’t know Anatoley was famous. I’m afraid I don’t know where he went. We’re not that close.”

“Is there anyone else we could contact? Family, maybe?” Lin asked.

The man thought for a moment. “He used to live with his son. His son moved out a couple of years ago to be closer to work, I think. I don’t have his number, though.”

“That’s still helpful. Thanks for your time.” Lin smiled, and the neighbor said goodbye before setting off.

“A son, huh?” Nadya opened the registry and searched for someone with the same family name. One result came up. She chuckled. “Dmitrey Anatoleyvich Ruzhin. It runs in the family.”

Lin leaned over to see the screen. Nadya lowered it for her—and noticed something else.

“Wait. Isn’t his place close to the scrapyard?”

Luka turned from his thoughts and came to look. “Yeah. Really close.”

“Then we should visit him,” Nadya said.

“Do you think he had something to do with it?” Lin asked.

“Maybe. But we’re not going in assuming he did. We hear him out first.” Nadya gave the house one last look before they headed to the bus stop.

It took nearly forty minutes by bus to reach Dmitrey’s apartment. The building was part of a renovation project completed about a decade earlier, and it looked new beside the older blocks around it. At the entrance, the automated reception system sat dark behind its counter. A volunteer was covering for it, while cleaners moved through the lobby by hand. Autonomous systems had been standard when the building opened; now, after the Ripple, people were filling in where the machines couldn’t.

The building had a separate lift for delivery robots, plus an entire floor of shared amenities: an indoor gym, game room, music room, art room, and other recreational spaces. All of it was free to Pogranichny’s residents. The building had long since run out of vacancies, and an extension was still under construction—now delayed by the Time Ripple.

“I wish my company housing was this nice,” Lin said with mock envy.

As a PRC citizen, her company provided her with housing in Pogranichny, but her apartment wasn’t nearly as fancy.

“My dorm’s old too. It’s not bad, but it could use some upgrades,” Luka said, trying to relate.

The institute’s funding likely went to research. Since the dorm was technically fine, extra comfort probably wasn’t high on the budget’s list.

“My house is at least sixty years old. It was my granddad’s, then my mom’s, then mine,” Nadya said.

She’d taken ownership of the house when she moved here almost two years ago. Her mother had moved to Vladivostok to live with her husband, leaving Nadya with a place to herself. Probably for the best, given her _Elektrifinadtsiya_ tendencies.

After a few more complaints about their housing, they checked the directory map and headed for the front desk. The receptionist was a human volunteer, filling in for the automated system after the Ripple.

“If you’re here about the room, it’s full,” he said, sounding annoyed. “There hasn’t been any further notice.” He seemed to have answered the same question all day; surely this was the kind of thing better left to a robot.

“No, we’re not here for that.” Nadya took out her Militsiya ID. “I’m Junior Lieutenant Morozova. I’m here to visit a resident named Dmitrey Ruzhin.”

Normally, an automated system would verify her identity and contact the station to confirm the visit. The volunteer barely seemed to care about the procedure. He started looking up the name.

“Dmitri—”

“With an ‘ey,’” Nadya cut in.

He rolled his eyes. “Dmitrey… so hip and cool, huh?”

He checked the listing and confirmed the match before handing Nadya a keycard. “Dmi—_Dmitrey_,” he said mockingly, “is in room 704. Please return the keycard when you’re done.”

“Thank you, comrade.” Nadya took the card and gestured for the others to follow her toward the elevator.

While they waited for the elevator, Luka glanced at Lin. “How are we going to approach him? Can we use your interview trick?”

“Not this time,” Lin said. “It worked on the neighbor because he wasn’t the person we needed answers from. Dmitrey can refuse to talk to us.”

Nadya stopped turning the keycard. “I could tell him his father’s been reported missing. That’s about as far as we can go. We’d need a warrant to search the apartment.”

Luka frowned. “Is that wise? You’re off-duty, and you’d be making the report up.”

Nadya’s jaw tightened. “Tarasov’s going to grill me for it. But we don’t have much time. First impressions matter. We need to sell it, make him believe this is an official follow-up so he’ll talk to us.”

The elevator arrived at the ground floor. A group of residents stepped out, and the three waited for them to clear the doorway before getting in. Once the doors closed and the elevator started up, Nadya pulled a fresh sheet of e-paper from her bag and synced it with her pocket terminal. Anatoley’s photograph and information appeared on the page. She began filling in the missing-person report with invented details.

When they reached the seventh floor, they followed the corridor to room 704. Before knocking, Nadya looked over the group and stopped.

“Lin, take off your coat. Give it to me.”

“W-Why?” Lin’s thoughts had already run ahead of the explanation.

“You look like a reporter. That might put him on edge.” Nadya shrugged out of her own coat and handed it over. “Wear this. I’ll hold yours.”

“Oh. Right. Sure.” Lin slipped into Nadya’s coat. It hung a little loose on her, but it was warm—almost too cozy, she thought, as she tugged the sleeves into place. Nadya gathered Lin’s coat over her arm, oblivious to the way Lin’s cheeks had warmed. Luka smiled to himself. Lin was hopelessly transparent; Nadya was completely oblivious.

“Should I take off my nameplate too?” he asked.

“Good point.” Nadya nodded. “From now on, you two are my trainees. Got it?”

Luka unclipped his nameplate and slipped it into his pocket. Nadya stepped back to look them over. “Ready?”

Luka and Lin nodded. Nadya took a breath. She couldn’t afford to mess this up; if Dmitrey turned them away, this lead might be gone. She raised her hand and knocked on the door…






 




